{"name":"Four Seasons Oolong","hex":"#6a7252","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"four","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["NATO & ICAO radiotelephony clear code (spelling-alphabet name) for the digit 4."]},{"part":"Numeral","definitions":["A numerical value equal to 4; the number following three and preceding five.","Describing a set or group with four elements."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/four"},{"term":"seasons","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of season"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of season"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seasons"},{"term":"oolong","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A partially fermented tea, often roasted, which combines the characteristics of green tea and black tea."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oolong"}],"entries":[{"title":"Season","blocks":[{"text":"A season is a division of the year based on changes in weather, ecology, and the number of daylight hours in a given region. On Earth, seasons are the result of the axial parallelism of Earth's tilted orbit around the Sun. In temperate and polar regions, the seasons are marked by changes in the intensity of sunlight that reaches the Earth's surface, variations of which may cause animals to undergo hibernation or to migrate, and plants to be dormant. Various cultures define the number and nature of seasons based on regional variations, and as such there are a number of both modern and historical definitions of the seasons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/330px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Season","wikidata":"Q24384"},{"title":"Oolong","blocks":[{"text":"Oolong is a traditional semi-oxidized Chinese tea produced through a process that includes withering the leaves under strong sun and allowing some oxidation to occur before curling and twisting. Most oolong teas, especially those of fine quality, involve unique tea plant cultivars that are exclusively used for particular varieties. The degree of oxidation, which is controlled by the length of time between picking and final drying, can range from 8% to 85% depending on the variety and production style. Oolong is especially popular in southeastern China and among ethnic Chinese in Southeast Asia, as is the Fujian preparation process known as the gongfu tea ceremony."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Oolong_tea_leaf.jpg/330px-Oolong_tea_leaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oolong","wikidata":"Q231587"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/500px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/1280px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/500px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The astronomical and meteorological periods of the four-seasons reckoning across the year at the Northern Hemisphere, displayed as segments of Earth's orbit. Astronomically a season encompasses the time between an equinox and solstice. Meteorologically seasons are quarters of the annual temperature cycle,[1] centered on the season characteristic temperatures, which trail equinox and solstice.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChangingSeasons_NH_01.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/North_season.jpg/500px-North_season.jpg?ut